Per-machine look, so a session on another machine reads as that machine even
inside this machine's terminal and tuios.

  hosts/<short hostname>   this machine's choices, one "key value" per line:
                             theme NAME   which theme below (default: kanagawa)
                             badge TEXT   a label in the fish prompt (default: none)
  NAME.fish                fish colours and prompt colours, sourced by fish/conf.d/theme.fish
  NAME.lua                 neovim colourscheme, run by nvim/lua/alex-config/host_theme.lua

A machine with no hosts/ file gets kanagawa and no badge. The short hostname is
what `prompt_hostname` prints in fish. Try a theme for one shell or nvim with
HOST_THEME=NAME.

Use hex colours in themes for other machines: named colours (blue, cyan) follow
the palette of whichever terminal draws them, so they cannot tell machines apart.
